<title>YUGOSLAVIA: YUGOSLAV JLY TRADE GAP $126 MLN VS JUNE'S $110 MLN.</title>
<headline>YUGOSLAV JLY TRADE GAP $126 MLN VS JUNE'S $110 MLN.</headline>
<dateline>BELGRADE 1997-08-08</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Federal Statistics Office</p>
<p>reported the following economic indicator details.</p>
<p> YUGOSLAV TRADE	 JULY	 JUNE	 JULY 96</p>
<p> Exports million USD   149	  156	   169</p>
<p> Imports		   275	  266	   348</p>
<p> Balance		 - 126	 -110	  -179</p>
<p>CUMULATIVE FIGURES (JANUARY TO JULY)</p>
<p> Exports million USD 1,192	1,028	   969</p>
<p> Imports		 2,481	2,112	 2,082</p>
<p> Balance		-1,289     -1,084	-1,113</p>
<p>NOTES -- July year-on-year exports dropped 11.5 percent and imports fell 20.9 percent on July 1996.</p>
<p>Cumulative January-July exports were up 23.0 percent and imports up 19.1 percent on the same seven-month period 1996.</p>
<p>Yugoslavia's 1996 trade gap stood at $2.26 billion, with exports of $1.842 billion and imports $4.102 billion.</p>
<p>Data refers to the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia,</p>
<p>comprising Serbia and Montenegro.</p>
<p>-- Amra Kevic, Belgrade newsroom +381 11 311 4305</p>
</text>
